**Hay fever** is an **allergy to pollen**, a fine powder from plants. When pollen gets into your nose, eyes or throat, your immune system overreacts. It affects around **1 in 4 people** in the UK.

## Symptoms

- sneezing and a **runny or blocked nose**
- **itchy, red or watery eyes**
- an itchy throat, mouth, nose or ears
- coughing
- headache and feeling tired
- wheezing, if you also have asthma

## Pollen seasons

- **tree pollen**: late March to mid May
- **grass pollen**: mid May to July. This is the most common cause
- **weed pollen**: end of June to September

Pollen counts are highest on warm, dry, windy days, and in the early morning and evening.

## Getting help

Most people can manage hay fever with advice and medicine from a **pharmacist**. See a GP if pharmacy medicines are not helping, your symptoms are severe, or you are wheezy.

Read about [hay fever treatment](/conditions/respiratory-and-allergy/hay-fever/treatment/).
